      "examAnswer": "Let G be a non-empty set with a binary operation *. The algebraic structure (G, *) is called a semigroup if * is closed and associative on G. A semigroup which also possesses an identity element e such that a * e = e * a = a for all a ∈ G is called a monoid. A monoid in which every element a ∈ G possesses an inverse a⁻¹ ∈ G such that a * a⁻¹ = a⁻¹ * a = e is called a group. A group in which the operation is further commutative, that is a * b = b * a for all a, b ∈ G, is called an abelian or commutative group. Thus every group is a monoid and every monoid is a semigroup, but the converse statements need not hold; for example (Z, ×) is a monoid but not a group, since 2 has no multiplicative inverse in Z.",
