      "examAnswer": "A group G is said to be cyclic if there exists an element a ∈ G such that every element of G can be expressed as an integral power of a, that is G = ⟨a⟩ = {aⁿ : n ∈ Z}; such an element a is called a generator of G. The order of an element a ∈ G is defined as the least positive integer n for which aⁿ = e, where e is the identity element. Every cyclic group is abelian, since aᵐ · aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ = aⁿ · aᵐ, though the converse is not true, the Klein four-group being abelian but not cyclic. Further, every subgroup of a cyclic group is cyclic, and every group of prime order is necessarily cyclic. For example, (Z₆, +₆) is cyclic of order 6 with generators 1 and 5.",
