      "examAnswer": "A permutation of a non-empty set S is a one-one and onto mapping from S onto itself. The set of all permutations of n distinct symbols forms a group under the operation of composition of mappings, called the symmetric group of degree n and denoted Sₙ. Its order is n!, the identity element is the identity permutation which leaves every symbol unchanged, and the inverse of a permutation is the permutation obtained by reversing each mapping. For n ≥ 3 the group Sₙ is non-abelian; for instance in S₃, (1 2) ∘ (1 3) = (1 3 2) whereas (1 3) ∘ (1 2) = (1 2 3). A subgroup of a symmetric group is called a permutation group, and by Cayley's theorem every finite group is isomorphic to some permutation group.",
