      "examAnswer": "Let (G, *) and (G′, ∘) be two groups. A mapping f : G → G′ is called a homomorphism if f(a * b) = f(a) ∘ f(b) for all a, b ∈ G, that is, if it preserves the group operation. Every homomorphism satisfies f(e) = e′ and f(a⁻¹) = [f(a)]⁻¹. The set ker f = {a ∈ G : f(a) = e′} is called the kernel of f and is always a normal subgroup of G, while the image of f is a subgroup of G′. A homomorphism which is both one-one and onto is called an isomorphism, and two groups connected by an isomorphism are said to be isomorphic, written G ≅ G′. A homomorphism is one-one if and only if its kernel consists of the identity element alone.",
